## Solution

**Claim.** The only entire functions $f$ satisfying $f\!\left(\tfrac{1}{n}\right)=f\!\left(\tfrac{1}{n^{2}}\right)$ for all $n\in\mathbb{N}$ are the constant functions.

**Proof.**

Define $g(z)=f(z)-f(z^{2})$. Since $f$ is entire and $z\mapsto z^{2}$ is entire, $g$ is entire. The hypothesis gives
$$g\!\left(\tfrac{1}{n}\right)=f\!\left(\tfrac{1}{n}\right)-f\!\left(\tfrac{1}{n^{2}}\right)=0\qquad\text{for every }n\in\mathbb{N}.$$

The set $\{1/n:n\in\mathbb{N}\}$ has an accumulation point at $0$, which lies in the interior of the domain of $g$. By the **identity theorem** for holomorphic functions, $g\equiv 0$ on $\mathbb{C}$. Hence
$$f(z)=f(z^{2})\qquad\text{for all }z\in\mathbb{C}.\tag{$\ast$}$$

Now expand $f$ as a power series about the origin (convergent on all of $\mathbb{C}$):
$$f(z)=\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}a_{k}z^{k}.$$
Then $f(z^{2})=\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}a_{k}z^{2k}$. Substituting into $(\ast)$ and comparing coefficients of $z^{k}$:

- For **odd** $k$: the right-hand side has no $z^{k}$ term, so $a_{k}=0$.
- For **even** $k=2m$ (with $m\ge 1$): $a_{2m}=a_{m}$.

We show $a_{k}=0$ for every $k\ge 1$. Given $k\ge 1$, write $k=2^{s}m$ with $m\ge 1$ odd and $s\ge 0$. Repeatedly applying the even-case relation,
$$a_{k}=a_{2^{s}m}=a_{2^{s-1}m}=\cdots=a_{m}.$$
Since $m\ge 1$ is odd, the odd-case relation gives $a_{m}=0$. Therefore $a_{k}=0$ for all $k\ge 1$.

It follows that $f(z)=a_{0}$, a constant. Conversely, every constant function trivially satisfies $f(1/n)=f(1/n^{2})$ for all $n$.

**Conclusion.** The entire functions satisfying the given condition are exactly the constant functions $f(z)\equiv c$, $c\in\mathbb{C}$. $\blacksquare$

